PILLARS = [
dict(slug="health", icon="🌿", area="Health", title="Health: the energy that pays for everything else",
desc="Sleep, movement and food in the order that gives the biggest return for the least effort.",
tool=("tools/sleep-calculator.html", "Sleep Calculator"),
take=["Sleep is the first lever: fix wake time before anything else.", "Walking counts. Most of the benefit of exercise arrives in the first 150 minutes a week.", "Eat mostly plants and protein you recognise; consistency beats any named diet."],
body="""
<p>Every other area of life is paid for in energy. A sharp mind, patient parenting, a demanding career and a good mood all draw from the same account, and that account is filled by three unglamorous deposits: sleep, movement and food. People tend to attack them in the reverse order of usefulness — starting with an ambitious diet, adding a punishing gym plan, and treating sleep as what is left over. Flip the order.</p>
<h2>1. Sleep first</h2>
<p>Most adults function well on seven to nine hours. Fewer than seven on a regular basis is associated with worse mood, weaker attention and poorer long-term health. The single most effective habit is not an earlier bedtime but a <strong>fixed wake-up time</strong>, seven days a week. Your body clock anchors to when you get up and see light; a stable morning makes you sleepy at a predictable hour at night.</p>
<ul><li>Get outdoor light within an hour of waking, even on a cloudy day.</li><li>Stop caffeine about eight hours before bed.</li><li>Keep the bedroom cool, dark and boring. Charge the phone in another room.</li></ul>
<h2>2. Move often, lift sometimes</h2>
<p>Global health guidance for adults converges on roughly 150 minutes of moderate activity a week — a brisk half-hour walk on five days — plus two sessions that challenge your muscles. The curve is steep at the start: going from nothing to a little delivers more benefit than going from a lot to slightly more. If you sit for work, the walk after lunch is the highest-value appointment in your calendar.</p>
<p>Strength matters more with every decade. Two short sessions a week of pushing, pulling, squatting and carrying is enough to keep muscle and bone, and with them your independence later in life.</p>
<h2>3. Eat like an adult who plans to be around</h2>
<p>Nutrition research is noisy, but the stable findings are boring: eat vegetables, fruit, beans, whole grains and nuts often; get enough protein; drink mostly water; keep ultra-processed snacks and sugary drinks occasional. You do not need a named diet. You need a default breakfast, a default lunch and three dinners you can cook without thinking.</p>
<h2>A 14-day starter</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Days 1–14:</strong> same wake-up time every day.</li><li><strong>Days 3–14:</strong> ten-minute walk after your largest meal.</li><li><strong>Days 7–14:</strong> one extra portion of vegetables at dinner.</li></ol>
<p>That is all. Resist adding more until these feel automatic. Then use the Life Score again and watch what moved.</p>
<p class="form-note">General information only. If you have a medical condition, are pregnant, or are starting exercise after a long break, speak with a qualified health professional first.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="mind", icon="🧠", area="Mind", title="Mind: calm is a skill, not a personality trait",
desc="Practical ways to lower daily stress, steady attention and feel more at ease — without becoming a monk.",
tool=("tools/breathing-timer.html", "Breathing Timer"),
take=["Stress is a body state first; change the body and the thoughts follow.", "Attention is trained by what you repeatedly do with your phone.", "Three good things, written nightly, is the cheapest mood practice there is."],
body="""
<p>Nobody is born calm. Calm people have, usually without noticing, built a set of habits that bring their nervous system back to baseline quickly. Those habits can be copied.</p>
<h2>Start with the body</h2>
<p>When you are stressed, your breathing gets fast and shallow, and that pattern tells the brain the threat is real. You can run the loop in reverse. Slow breathing with a longer exhale — for example four seconds in, six to eight out — nudges the body toward its rest state within a few minutes. It is not mystical; it is plumbing. Practise when you are not stressed so the skill is there when you are.</p>
<h2>Guard your attention</h2>
<p>A mind that is interrupted every few minutes never settles. You do not need a digital detox; you need friction:</p>
<ul><li>Turn off every notification that is not from a human being.</li><li>Move the most tempting apps off the first screen.</li><li>Keep one hour a day and the bedroom at night phone-free.</li></ul>
<p>Single-tasking feels slow and finishes faster. Try one thing at a time for a single hour and compare how you feel.</p>
<h2>Train what you notice</h2>
<p>The brain is tuned to spot problems. Left alone, it will give you an accurate list of everything wrong and call it realism. Deliberately noticing what went right rebalances the picture. Each night write down three things that went well and why they happened. Two weeks of this is one of the most consistently supported exercises in positive psychology.</p>
<h2>Know your early-warning signs</h2>
<p>Everyone has tells: snapping at small things, scrolling late, skipping meals, cancelling plans. Write yours down. When two appear in the same week, treat it as an instruction to simplify — drop one commitment, go to bed early, get outside.</p>
<h2>When self-help is not enough</h2>
<p>Low mood or anxiety that lasts more than two weeks, or that gets in the way of work, sleep or relationships, deserves professional support. Reaching out to a doctor or licensed therapist is not a failure of self-improvement; it is self-improvement. If you are ever in crisis or thinking about harming yourself, contact your local emergency number or a crisis line in your country right away.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="relationships", icon="💛", area="Love", title="Relationships: the strongest predictor of a good life",
desc="What long-running research says about connection — and the small weekly habits that build it.",
tool=("challenges.html", "30-Day Connection Challenge"),
take=["Close relationships predict long-term wellbeing better than income or status.", "Connection is built from frequency, not grand gestures.", "Listening well is the rarest and most valued social skill."],
body="""
<p>When researchers follow people across decades and ask what separated those who thrived from those who did not, the answer is consistent and a little inconvenient for anyone hoping for a clever hack: warm, dependable relationships. Not the number of friends, and not being in a couple — the presence of people you can count on, and who can count on you.</p>
<h2>Frequency beats intensity</h2>
<p>Friendships fade through neglect far more often than through conflict. Adults lose touch because nothing in their calendar makes contact automatic. Fix the structure and the feeling follows:</p>
<ul><li>One recurring weekly slot — a walk, a call, a meal — with someone who matters.</li><li>One message a day to a person you have not spoken to in a month. Thirty seconds, no agenda.</li><li>One standing monthly gathering that happens whether or not everyone can come.</li></ul>
<h2>Be the one who listens</h2>
<p>Most people in a conversation are waiting to speak. If you ask a real question, stay quiet for the answer and then ask one more, you will be remembered as unusually good company. Put the phone out of sight; its mere presence on the table lowers the quality of the exchange.</p>
<h2>Say the appreciative thing out loud</h2>
<p>We assume the people close to us know what we value about them. Usually they do not. Specific appreciation — "I noticed you stayed late to help me finish that, it meant a lot" — costs nothing and is remembered for years.</p>
<h2>Repair quickly</h2>
<p>Strong relationships are not the ones without friction; they are the ones where repair happens fast. A plain "I was short with you earlier, I am sorry" within the day prevents the slow build-up that actually ends relationships.</p>
<h2>If you are starting from lonely</h2>
<p>Loneliness is common and nothing to be ashamed of. The reliable route out is repeated, low-pressure contact around a shared activity: a class, a volunteer shift, a running group, a faith community. Go to the same thing at the same time for six weeks. Familiarity does the work that confidence cannot.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="career", icon="🛠️", area="Work", title="Work: build skills that compound",
desc="How to make work more rewarding, more valuable and less consuming — at any stage of a career.",
tool=("tools/focus-timer.html", "Focus Timer"),
take=["Rare and useful skills buy autonomy; autonomy is what makes work satisfying.", "One protected hour of deep work a day outperforms a scattered eight.", "Review your direction every 90 days, not every five years."],
body="""
<p>"Follow your passion" is poor advice for most people because most people do not have a pre-existing passion that also pays. A more reliable pattern runs the other way: get good at something useful, use that leverage to gain control over what you work on and how, and enjoyment tends to follow competence and autonomy.</p>
<h2>Pick a skill and go deep</h2>
<p>List the three skills that would make you clearly more valuable in your field in two years. Choose one. Give it three focused hours a week for ninety days. Deliberate practice — working at the edge of your ability with feedback — is uncomfortable, which is exactly why few people do it and why it pays.</p>
<h2>Protect one hour</h2>
<p>Knowledge work rewards concentration, and modern workplaces are built to destroy it. Block one hour early in the day, close email and chat, and work on the single most valuable task. A timer helps: fifty minutes on, ten off. One such hour daily is roughly 250 hours of your best work each year.</p>
<h2>Make your work visible</h2>
<p>Good work that nobody knows about does not create opportunity. Once a week, write three lines: what you shipped, what you learned, what is next. Share it with your manager or team. Over a year this becomes the evidence for a raise, a promotion or a new role.</p>
<h2>Set an off-switch</h2>
<p>A shutdown routine — review tomorrow's list, close every tab, say out loud that you are done — makes it possible to rest without guilt. Rest is not the opposite of productivity; it is where the next day's attention comes from.</p>
<h2>Run a 90-day review</h2>
<p>Every quarter, answer four questions in writing: What gave me energy? What drained it? What am I getting better at? What would I do if I were not afraid? Careers are rarely changed by one dramatic leap. They are steered by small corrections made often.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="money", icon="💰", area="Money", title="Money: enough, on autopilot",
desc="A calm, jargon-free system for spending less than you earn and letting time do the heavy lifting.",
tool=("tools/compound-growth.html", "Compound Growth Calculator"),
take=["The gap between income and spending matters more than either number.", "Automate saving on payday so willpower is never involved.", "Time in the market does most of the work — start small, start now."],
body="""
<p>Money is not the goal of a good life, but money stress can crowd out everything that is. The aim of this guide is modest and powerful: get your finances to the point where they run quietly in the background.</p>
<h2>Step 1 — Know your number</h2>
<p>For thirty days, write down everything you spend. Do not judge it or change it; just look. Most people find two or three categories that are much larger than they believed and that they do not especially value. That is your slack.</p>
<h2>Step 2 — Build a buffer</h2>
<p>Before investing, hold a small emergency fund in an easy-access account. Start with one month of essential expenses and build toward three to six. The buffer is not there to earn a return; it is there so a car repair is an inconvenience instead of a crisis.</p>
<h2>Step 3 — Pay yourself first, automatically</h2>
<p>Set a transfer for the day your pay arrives. Savings that leave before you see them are never missed; savings that depend on what is left at month-end rarely happen. Begin with an amount so small it is slightly embarrassing, and raise it every time your income rises.</p>
<h2>Step 4 — Clear expensive debt</h2>
<p>High-interest debt compounds against you faster than most investments compound for you. List your debts by interest rate and send every spare unit of money to the highest one while paying minimums on the rest.</p>
<h2>Step 5 — Let compounding work</h2>
<p>Growth on growth is slow for years and then startling. The compound growth calculator on this site shows the shape: the last decade of a long plan typically adds more than the first two combined. The practical lesson is that <em>when</em> you start matters more than <em>how much</em> you start with.</p>
<h2>What money is for</h2>
<p>Research on spending and wellbeing points the same way: beyond covering needs, money improves life most when it buys time, experiences and things for other people. Decide what "enough" means for you. Without that number, no amount will feel like it.</p>
<p class="form-note">This is general education, not personal financial advice. Products, taxes and protections differ by country — consider speaking with a licensed adviser about your situation.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="growth", icon="📚", area="Growth", title="Growth: get one percent better on purpose",
desc="How habits actually form, why motivation fails, and the simple weekly review that keeps you improving.",
tool=("tools/habit-tracker.html", "Habit Tracker"),
take=["Shrink the habit until it is too small to skip.", "Attach new habits to things you already do.", "A 15-minute weekly review is the habit that maintains all the others."],
body="""
<p>Personal growth has a marketing problem: it is sold as transformation, but it is delivered as repetition. The people who change the most are rarely the most motivated. They are the ones who made the desired behaviour easy and then kept showing up.</p>
<h2>Make it tiny</h2>
<p>A habit that needs motivation will fail on the first bad day. Scale it down until it sounds almost silly: one push-up, one page, one sentence in a journal. The purpose of the tiny version is not the result; it is to become the kind of person who does not miss. Volume can be added later. Consistency cannot.</p>
<h2>Anchor it</h2>
<p>New behaviours stick best when attached to an existing routine. Use the formula <em>"After I [current habit], I will [new habit]."</em> After I pour my coffee, I will read one page. After I close my laptop, I will write tomorrow's top task. The old habit becomes the reminder, so you do not rely on memory.</p>
<h2>Shape the room</h2>
<p>Environment beats willpower. Put the book on the pillow, the fruit on the counter, the running shoes by the door. Put the biscuits on a high shelf and the phone charger in the hallway. Every step of friction you add or remove changes behaviour more than a resolution does.</p>
<h2>Track it and never miss twice</h2>
<p>A simple tick on a calendar is surprisingly motivating — nobody wants to break a streak. But streaks break. The rule that matters is: never miss twice. One missed day is an accident; two is the start of a new habit.</p>
<h2>The weekly review</h2>
<p>Fifteen minutes, same time each week, three questions:</p>
<ol><li>What worked this week? <em>Keep it.</em></li><li>What did not? <em>Stop it or shrink it.</em></li><li>What is the one thing that would make next week better? <em>Start it.</em></li></ol>
<p>This is the keystone. Without a review, improvement depends on luck. With one, you are running small experiments on your own life and keeping the winners.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="play", icon="🎈", area="Play", title="Play: rest is part of the work",
desc="Why fun, hobbies and real time off are not rewards for productivity but the source of it.",
tool=("tools/gratitude-journal.html", "Gratitude Journal"),
take=["Leisure you actively choose restores more than leisure that just happens to you.", "A hobby with no purpose is a feature, not a flaw.", "Anticipation is half the pleasure — plan small things to look forward to."],
body="""
<p>Ask adults what they do for fun and many will describe how they recover: television, scrolling, sleep. Recovery matters, but it is not the same as play. Play is anything you do for its own sake, where the outcome does not matter and time moves differently. A life with none of it can be efficient and still feel flat.</p>
<h2>Active beats passive</h2>
<p>Passive leisure asks nothing of you and gives little back. Active leisure — making something, moving, playing a game, learning a tune — demands a little effort up front and returns far more energy. A useful rule: after work, do something active <em>before</em> you sit down. Once you are on the sofa, the evening is decided.</p>
<h2>Protect a pointless hobby</h2>
<p>Modern culture pushes us to monetise every interest. Resist. Have at least one thing you are happily mediocre at and have no plans to turn into a side business. The absence of stakes is what makes it restful.</p>
<h2>Chase absorption</h2>
<p>The state of being completely absorbed — often called flow — shows up when a task is just hard enough to demand your full attention and gives you quick feedback. Climbing, cooking a new recipe, painting, chess, gardening and playing music all qualify. Notice which activities make you forget your phone, and schedule more of them.</p>
<h2>Take real time off</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Daily:</strong> one hour that is nobody's but yours.</li><li><strong>Weekly:</strong> one half-day with no obligations and no email.</li><li><strong>Yearly:</strong> at least one stretch long enough that you stop thinking about work — for most people that takes more than a week.</li></ul>
<h2>Plan small joys</h2>
<p>Looking forward to something lifts mood for days before it happens. You do not need a holiday; a Friday film, a Sunday market, a new walking route will do. Put one small thing on the calendar every week and let anticipation do its quiet work.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="purpose", icon="🧭", area="Purpose", title="Purpose: decide what matters, then act like it",
desc="A practical way to name your values, find direction and feel that your days add up to something.",
tool=("tools/goal-builder.html", "Goal Builder"),
take=["Purpose is usually built through action, not discovered through thought.", "Values are choices about how to behave, not goals to finish.", "Contribution is the most dependable source of meaning."],
body="""
<p>Purpose sounds like something you find, fully formed, on a mountain. For most people it is assembled slowly from three plain materials: knowing what you value, using your strengths, and being useful to someone else.</p>
<h2>Name your values</h2>
<p>A value is not a goal. "Run a marathon" is a goal; "being healthy and disciplined" is a value. Goals finish; values are directions you can keep walking in for life. Write down ten things that matter to you — honesty, family, craft, freedom, learning, service, adventure — then cut the list to five, then rank them. The ranking is where it gets real, because most hard decisions are conflicts between two good things.</p>
<h2>Check the calendar, not the mirror</h2>
<p>Your real priorities are visible in how you spend time and money. Put your top five values next to last week's calendar. Where they match, you probably felt good. Where they clash, you probably felt the low-grade unease people describe as "something is off". Close one gap this month.</p>
<h2>Use the four-circle test</h2>
<p>A well-known framing asks you to look for the overlap of four things: what you enjoy, what you are good at, what the world needs, and what you can be paid for. Few people land in the centre, and you do not have to. Your job can cover two circles while a side project or volunteering covers the others.</p>
<h2>Contribute</h2>
<p>Across cultures, the feeling that life is meaningful rises when people help others. It does not have to be grand. Mentor one person. Give two hours a month to a local cause. Be reliably kind to the people already around you. Meaning is less about how big the contribution is than about knowing who it is for.</p>
<h2>Write the ordinary day</h2>
<p>Describe, in one paragraph, a great <em>ordinary</em> Tuesday five years from now: where you wake up, who is there, what you work on, how the evening goes. Not a fantasy — a day you could repeat. It is a clearer compass than any five-year plan, and you can start moving toward parts of it this week.</p>
"""),
]

ARTICLES = [
dict(slug="morning-routine", area="Health", title="A 20-minute morning routine that actually survives real life",
desc="Light, water, movement and one decision — a short routine you can keep on your worst day.",
body="""<p>Elaborate morning routines look good online and collapse the first time a child wakes early. A routine worth having is short enough to survive bad days.</p>
<h2>The four pieces</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Light (5 min).</strong> Get outside or to a bright window soon after waking. Morning light sets your body clock and makes it easier to fall asleep that night.</li><li><strong>Water (1 min).</strong> You wake up mildly dehydrated. Drink a glass before coffee.</li><li><strong>Movement (10 min).</strong> A walk, some stretching, a few squats and push-ups. The goal is to feel awake, not to train.</li><li><strong>One decision (4 min).</strong> Write the single task that would make today a success. Do it before opening email if you can.</li></ol>
<h2>What to leave out</h2>
<p>Your phone. Checking messages first thing hands your attention to other people's priorities before you have chosen your own. Give yourself twenty minutes first.</p>
<h2>Make it stick</h2>
<p>Set everything up the night before: glass by the sink, shoes by the door, notebook open. Then anchor the sequence to something that already happens — your alarm, the kettle, the dog. If you miss a day, do the two-minute version: light and water. Never zero.</p>"""),
dict(slug="walking-benefits", area="Health", title="Walking is underrated: what a daily walk does for body and mind",
desc="Why the humble walk may be the highest-return health habit — and how to fit one in.",
body="""<p>Walking has no equipment, no learning curve and almost no injury risk, which is probably why it is taken less seriously than it deserves.</p>
<h2>What regular walking is linked to</h2>
<ul><li>Better heart health and blood-sugar control, especially when you walk after meals.</li><li>Improved mood and lower stress within a single session.</li><li>Better sleep, particularly when the walk is outdoors in daylight.</li><li>Clearer thinking — many people find ideas come more easily on the move.</li></ul>
<h2>How much?</h2>
<p>You do not need a specific step count. Benefits rise steadily from low levels, and a brisk thirty minutes on most days meets common activity guidelines. "Brisk" means you can talk but not sing.</p>
<h2>Fitting it in</h2>
<ul><li>Ten minutes after each main meal adds up to thirty.</li><li>Take phone calls on foot.</li><li>Get off one stop early or park at the far end.</li><li>Make one weekly catch-up with a friend a walk instead of a coffee.</li></ul>
<p>Start where you are. If ten minutes is your current limit, ten minutes is the plan.</p>"""),
dict(slug="digital-declutter", area="Mind", title="Digital declutter: take back two hours a day",
desc="A one-evening reset for your phone that lowers distraction without deleting your life.",
body="""<p>The average smartphone is configured by companies whose revenue depends on your attention. One evening of reconfiguration changes the balance.</p>
<h2>The 45-minute reset</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Notifications:</strong> turn off everything except calls, messages from real people and calendar alerts.</li><li><strong>Home screen:</strong> keep only tools — maps, camera, notes, calendar. Move social, news and video apps into a folder on the last page.</li><li><strong>Log out</strong> of the two apps you open most on autopilot. The extra login step is often enough.</li><li><strong>Greyscale:</strong> try a colour-free screen for a week. It is remarkable how dull the feed becomes.</li><li><strong>Charger:</strong> move it out of the bedroom and buy a cheap alarm clock.</li></ol>
<h2>Replace, do not just remove</h2>
<p>An empty gap gets refilled by the old habit. Decide in advance what you will do in the moments you used to scroll: a book by the sofa, a puzzle on the table, a short walk.</p>
<h2>Set two check-in windows</h2>
<p>Pick two fixed times a day for social media and news. Outside them, the apps stay closed. Most people report that nothing important was missed — and that evenings feel longer.</p>"""),
dict(slug="stress-reset", area="Mind", title="The five-minute stress reset you can do anywhere",
desc="A simple sequence — breathe, name, move, choose — for the moment stress spikes.",
body="""<p>Stress is useful in short bursts and corrosive when it never switches off. This sequence takes five minutes and needs nothing but a little privacy.</p>
<h2>1. Breathe (2 minutes)</h2>
<p>Inhale through the nose for four counts, exhale slowly for six to eight. A longer out-breath signals safety to the body. Use the Breathing Timer if you want a guide.</p>
<h2>2. Name it (1 minute)</h2>
<p>Say, silently or on paper, exactly what you feel: "I am anxious about the deadline," not "everything is a mess." Putting a precise label on an emotion tends to reduce its intensity.</p>
<h2>3. Move (1 minute)</h2>
<p>Stand, roll your shoulders, shake out your hands, walk to a window. Stress prepares the body to act; a little movement lets some of that energy go.</p>
<h2>4. Choose one next step (1 minute)</h2>
<p>Ask: what is the smallest useful action I can take in the next ten minutes? Then do only that. Overwhelm shrinks when it meets a specific task.</p>
<p class="form-note">If stress is constant or you feel unable to cope, please talk to a health professional.</p>"""),
dict(slug="active-listening", area="Love", title="How to really listen: five habits of people others love talking to",
desc="Listening is a learnable skill. These five habits make every conversation better.",
body="""<p>Being listened to is so rare that people remember who does it. These habits are simple and take practice.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Remove the phone.</strong> Not face-down — out of sight. Half-attention is felt immediately.</li><li><strong>Let silence work.</strong> When someone pauses, count to two before speaking. The most important thing often comes after the pause.</li><li><strong>Ask a second question.</strong> "What was that like?" or "Then what happened?" shows you were following, and takes the conversation somewhere real.</li><li><strong>Reflect before you respond.</strong> "It sounds like you felt overlooked" lets the other person confirm or correct — either way they feel understood.</li><li><strong>Hold the advice.</strong> Ask, "Do you want ideas, or do you want me to just listen?" Most of the time it is the second.</li></ol>
<h2>Try it tonight</h2>
<p>In your next conversation, aim to speak for less than a third of the time. Notice how much more you learn and how the other person responds.</p>"""),
dict(slug="making-friends-as-an-adult", area="Love", title="Making friends as an adult: a realistic plan",
desc="Why adult friendship is hard, and a repeatable way to build it anyway.",
body="""<p>School gave you friends by default: the same people, in the same place, week after week. Adult life removes that structure, so you have to rebuild it deliberately.</p>
<h2>The three ingredients</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Proximity:</strong> you see each other without much effort.</li><li><strong>Repetition:</strong> you see each other often.</li><li><strong>A setting that lets guards drop:</strong> a shared activity gives you something to talk about.</li></ul>
<h2>The plan</h2>
<ol><li>Choose one recurring group activity you would enjoy even if you met no one — a class, club, team, choir, volunteer shift.</li><li>Commit to six consecutive sessions. Familiarity builds slowly and then quickly.</li><li>Learn two names each time and use them the next week.</li><li>Be the one who suggests the next step: "A few of us are getting coffee after — join?"</li><li>Follow up within two days with something specific from the conversation.</li></ol>
<p>Expect it to feel awkward. Almost everyone in the room wants more connection too; someone has to go first.</p>"""),
dict(slug="deep-work-basics", area="Work", title="Deep work basics: how to concentrate in a distracted world",
desc="A four-rule setup for doing an hour of your most valuable work every day.",
body="""<p>The ability to concentrate without distraction is becoming rarer at exactly the moment it is becoming more valuable. You can train it like a muscle.</p>
<h2>Rule 1 — Schedule it</h2>
<p>Deep work does not happen in leftover time. Put a block in the calendar, ideally at the same time each day, and treat it as a meeting with your most important client: you.</p>
<h2>Rule 2 — Define the output</h2>
<p>"Work on the report" invites drifting. "Draft sections one and two" gives the session a finish line.</p>
<h2>Rule 3 — Remove the exits</h2>
<p>Close email and chat. Put the phone in another room. Keep a notepad for stray thoughts so you can capture them without acting on them.</p>
<h2>Rule 4 — Rest properly</h2>
<p>Concentration is finite. Work in blocks of 25–50 minutes with a real break — stand, look far away, drink water. Do not spend the break on a screen.</p>
<p>Start with one 25-minute block a day and add time weekly. The Focus Timer keeps count for you.</p>"""),
dict(slug="saying-no", area="Work", title="The art of saying no without burning bridges",
desc="Scripts and principles for declining requests politely so you can say yes to what matters.",
body="""<p>Every yes is a no to something else — usually your own priorities. Declining well is a professional skill.</p>
<h2>Three principles</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Be fast.</strong> A quick no is kinder than a slow maybe.</li><li><strong>Be clear.</strong> "I can't take this on" is better than "I'll try to find time."</li><li><strong>Be brief.</strong> Long explanations invite negotiation.</li></ul>
<h2>Scripts you can borrow</h2>
<ul><li>"Thanks for thinking of me. I don't have the capacity to do this well right now, so I'll pass."</li><li>"I can't do that, but I can do this smaller thing by Friday — would that help?"</li><li>"If I take this on, the other project will slip. Which should I prioritise?" (for a manager)</li><li>"Let me check my commitments and reply tomorrow." (to buy time, then actually reply)</li></ul>
<h2>Check your default</h2>
<p>If your first instinct is always yes, set a personal rule: no new commitments without sleeping on it. The pause alone filters out half of what you would later regret.</p>"""),
dict(slug="emergency-fund", area="Money", title="How to build an emergency fund from zero",
desc="A step-by-step plan for your first financial buffer, even on a tight budget.",
body="""<p>An emergency fund turns a crisis into an inconvenience. It is the foundation every other money goal stands on.</p>
<h2>Set the first target low</h2>
<p>Forget six months of expenses for now. Aim for a starter buffer equal to one typical unexpected bill — a repair, a medical cost, an urgent trip. Hitting a small target builds belief.</p>
<h2>Find the money</h2>
<ol><li>Review subscriptions and cancel what you would not buy again today.</li><li>Pick one spending category to trim by a third for ninety days.</li><li>Send any windfall — refund, bonus, gift — straight to the fund.</li><li>Sell three things you no longer use.</li></ol>
<h2>Keep it separate and boring</h2>
<p>Use an easy-access savings account at a different bank from your everyday one. Out of sight helps. Name the account "Emergency" so future-you thinks twice.</p>
<h2>Automate, then grow</h2>
<p>Set a small automatic transfer for payday. When the starter target is reached, keep going toward one month of essentials, then three. If you have to use it, that is what it is for — refill it without guilt.</p>
<p class="form-note">General education only, not financial advice.</p>"""),
dict(slug="spend-on-what-matters", area="Money", title="Spend on what matters: a values-based budget in 30 minutes",
desc="A simple method to cut costs you don't care about and fund the things you love.",
body="""<p>Budgets fail when they feel like punishment. A values-based budget does the opposite: it spends generously on what you love by cutting hard on what you do not.</p>
<h2>The 30-minute exercise</h2>
<ol><li><strong>List last month's spending</strong> by category.</li><li><strong>Mark each category</strong> with a plus (adds real joy or value), a zero (necessary), or a minus (habit, not happiness).</li><li><strong>Cut the minuses</strong> without mercy. This is where the money is.</li><li><strong>Optimise the zeros</strong> once a year — insurance, phone plan, energy supplier.</li><li><strong>Protect the pluses.</strong> Budget for them on purpose and enjoy them guilt-free.</li></ol>
<h2>A simple structure</h2>
<p>Many people like a three-bucket split as a starting point: roughly half of take-home pay on needs, about a third on wants, and the rest on saving and debt. Adjust to your situation; the principle is that saving has its own bucket and is paid first.</p>
<p>Review monthly for the first three months. After that, the system largely runs itself.</p>"""),
dict(slug="reading-habit", area="Growth", title="How to read more: build a reading habit that lasts",
desc="Read a book a month with ten pages a day and a few environment tweaks.",
body="""<p>People who read a lot are not faster readers. They have just arranged their lives so that reading is the easy option.</p>
<h2>Ten pages a day</h2>
<p>Ten pages takes about fifteen minutes and adds up to roughly twelve average-length books a year. Attach it to a fixed moment — with morning coffee or just before sleep.</p>
<h2>Make books unavoidable</h2>
<ul><li>Keep a book wherever you usually pick up your phone: bedside, sofa, bag.</li><li>Always have the next book ready before finishing the current one.</li><li>Carry something to read whenever you might wait.</li></ul>
<h2>Quit freely</h2>
<p>Nothing kills a reading habit like a book you feel obliged to finish. If it has not earned your interest after fifty pages, stop. Life is short and the library is large.</p>
<h2>Remember more</h2>
<p>After each session, write one sentence about what you read. When you finish a book, write three ideas you want to keep. Explaining a book to a friend is the best memory tool of all.</p>"""),
dict(slug="finding-direction", area="Purpose", title="Feeling lost? Seven questions that point to your direction",
desc="A short journaling exercise for when you don't know what you want.",
body="""<p>Not knowing what you want is uncomfortable and completely normal. Clarity rarely arrives by thinking harder; it comes from asking better questions and then testing the answers.</p>
<h2>Take twenty minutes and write</h2>
<ol><li>When did I last lose track of time? What was I doing?</li><li>What do people regularly thank me for or ask my help with?</li><li>What did I love doing at ten years old, before anyone was grading it?</li><li>What problem in the world makes me genuinely angry or sad?</li><li>Whose life do I admire — and what exactly about it?</li><li>If money and opinion were not factors, what would I try for one year?</li><li>What am I tolerating that I know I should change?</li></ol>
<h2>Look for the pattern</h2>
<p>Read your answers the next day and underline words that repeat. Themes — teaching, building, caring, exploring, organising — matter more than job titles.</p>
<h2>Run a small experiment</h2>
<p>Pick the theme that pulls hardest and design a two-week test that costs little: a short course, a volunteer shift, a conversation with someone doing it. Direction is found by moving, then adjusting.</p>"""),
]
